"Speeding Up Sum-Check Proving (https://eprint.iacr.org/2025/1117)\nciteturn2view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://eprint.iacr.org/2025/1117\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 82\nL0: # What a lovely hat\nL1: #### Is it made out of 【0†tin foil†iacr.org】?\nL2: \nL3: 【1†Cryptology ePrint Archive】\nL4: \nL5:   * 【2†Papers】 Updates from the last:\nL6:     * 【3†7 days】\nL7:     * 【4†31 days】\nL8:     * 【5†6 months】\nL9:     * 【6†365 days】\nL10:     * * * *\nL11: \nL12:     * 【7†Listing by year】\nL13:     * 【8†All papers】\nL14:     * 【9†Compact view】\nL15:     * 【10†Subscribe†www.iacr.org】\nL16:     * * * *\nL17: \nL18:     * 【11†How to cite】\nL19:     * * * *\nL20:     * 【12†Harvesting metadata】\nL21:   * 【2†Submissions】\nL22:     * 【13†Submit a paper】\nL23:     * 【14†Revise or withdraw a paper】\nL24:     * 【15†Acceptance and publishing conditions】\nL25: \nL26: 【16†Image: Search Button】\nL27: \nL28: [Input] [Button: Search ]\nL29: #### Paper 2025/1117\nL30: \nL31: ### 【17†Speeding Up Sum-Check Proving】\nL32: \nL33: Suyash Bagad, Ingonyama\nL34: \nL35: Quang Dao, Carnegie Mellon University\nL36: \nL37: Yuval Domb, Ingonyama\nL38: \nL39: Justin Thaler, Georgetown University, a16z crypto research\nL40: ##### Abstract\nL41: At the core of the fastest known SNARKs is the sum-check protocol. In this paper, we describe two complementary optimizations that significantly accelerate sum-check proving in key applications. The first targets scenarios where polynomial evaluations involve small values, such as unsigned 32-bit integers or elements of small subfields within larger extension fields.\nL42: This setting is common in applications such as Jolt, a state-of-the-art zero-knowledge virtual machine (zkVM) built on the sum-check protocol. Our core idea is to replace expensive multiplications over large fields with cheaper operations over smaller domains, yielding both asymptotic speedups and significant constant-factor improvements.\nL43: The second optimization addresses a common pattern where sum-check is applied to polynomials of the form $g(x) = \\mathsf{eq}(r, x) \\cdot p(x)$, where $\\mathsf{eq}$ is the multilinear extension of the equality function. We present a technique that substantially reduces the prover's cost associated with the equality polynomial component. We also describe how to combine both optimizations, which is essential for applications like Spartan within Jolt.\nL44: We have implemented and integrated our optimizations into the Jolt zkVM. Our benchmarks show consistent $2\\text{-}3\\times$ speedups for proving the first sum-check of Spartan within Jolt, with performance gains reaching 20$\\times$ or more when baseline methods approach their memory limits.\nL45: Note: This paper combines and extends the results from two prior pre-prints, 2024/1046 and 2024/1210.\nL46: ##### Metadata\nL47: \nL48: Available format(s)\nL49:     【17†PDF】\nL50: Category\nL51:     【18†Applications】\nL52: Publication info\nL53:     Preprint.\nL54: Keywords\nL55:     【19†snark】【20†sum-check】【21†zkvm】\nL56: Contact author(s)\nL57:     suyash @ ingonyama com\nL58: qvd @ andrew cmu edu\nL59: yuval @ ingonyama com\nL60: justin r thaler @ gmail com\nL61: History\nL62:     2025-06-17: revised\nL63:     2025-06-13: received\nL64:     【22†See all versions】\nL65: Short URL\nL66:     【23†https://ia.cr/2025/1117†ia.cr】\nL67: License\nL68: \nL69: 【24†CC BY†creativecommons.org】\nL70: BibTeX 【25†Image】Copy to clipboard\nL71: \nL72:     @misc{cryptoeprint:2025/1117,\nL73:           author = {Suyash Bagad and Quang Dao and Yuval Domb and Justin Thaler},\nL74:           title = {Speeding Up Sum-Check Proving},\nL75:           howpublished = {Cryptology {ePrint} Archive, Paper 2025/1117},\nL76:           year = {2025},\nL77:           url = {https://eprint.iacr.org/2025/1117}\nL78:     }\nL79: \nL80: \nL81: Note: In order to protect the privacy of readers, eprint.iacr.org does not use cookies or embedded third party content.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2610.37710] zkDuel: Characterizing SumCheck Prover Kernels across GPU Programming Models (https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.37710)\nciteturn2view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.37710\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 164\nL0: 【0†Search】 【1†Submit】 【2†Donate†info.arxiv.org】 【3†Log in】\nL1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · 【4†Advanced search】\nL5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Hardware Architecture\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 26 Oct 2026]\nL9: # Title:zkDuel: Characterizing SumCheck Prover Kernels across GPU Programming Models\nL10: \nL11: Authors:【5†Samarth Narang】, 【6†Alhad Daftardar】, 【7†Brandon Reagen】\nL12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled zkDuel: Characterizing SumCheck Prover Kernels across GPU Programming Models, by Samarth Narang and 2 other authors\nL14: \nL15: 【8†View PDF】 【9†HTML (experimental)】\nL16: > Abstract:SumCheck is a central prover primitive in zero-knowledge proofs, exposing table-parallel work that maps naturally to GPUs but becomes increasingly arithmetic-intensive as finite fields widen. We study how this shift affects the cost of programming SumCheck in Triton rather than CUDA.\nL17: Our benchmark, zkDuel, provides matched implementations of 25 zkPHIRE-derived constraints alongside diagnostic polynomials, covering 32-, 64-, 128-, and 256-bit prime fields, with independent CPU-reference validation and cross-backend checksum gating. Of 800 configurations attempted on an NVIDIA H100, 758 complete with matching checksums.\nL18: CUDA's median advantage over Triton falls from 3.5x at 32 bits to 1.4x at 256 bits, and Triton is faster on 19 large-table configurations; the same convergence appears on an A100, where the gap falls from 4.4x to 1.6x. Profiling shows that the small-field gap largely reflects host launch overhead and redundant modular additions in Triton's original block reduction, whose relative costs diminish as multi-limb Montgomery work grows.\nL19: Replacing that reduction with 【10†this http URL†tl.reduce】 brings Triton's median kernel time within 8% of CUDA on both GPUs, while specialized point interpolation improves 256-bit evaluation kernels by 1.55-1.75x. At cryptographic widths, these results show that a Python-embedded DSL can approach CUDA performance, although the largest fully unrolled constraints still expose compiler-scalability limits.\nL20: Comments:  | Accepted to HASP'26 at MICRO 2026\nL21: Subjects:  | Hardware Architecture (cs.AR)\nL22: Cite as:  | 【11†arXiv:2610.37710】 [cs.AR]\nL23:    | (or 【12†arXiv:2610.37710v1】 [cs.AR] for this version)\nL24:    | 【13†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2610.37710†doi.org】 arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL25: Related DOI (Digital Object Identifier):  | 【14†https://doi.org/10.1145/3857307.3857312†doi.org】 DOI(s) linking to related resources\nL26: ## Submission history\nL27: \nL28: From: Samarth Narang [【15†view email】]\nL29: [v1] Mon, 26 Oct 2026 21:28:29 UTC (51 KB)\nL30: \nL31: Full-text links:\nL32: \nL33: ## Access Paper:\nL34: \nL35: View a PDF of the paper titled zkDuel: Characterizing SumCheck Prover Kernels across GPU Programming Models, by Samarth Narang and 2 other authors\nL36: \nL37:   * 【8†View PDF】\nL38:   * 【9†HTML (experimental)】\nL39:   * 【16†TeX Source】\nL40: \nL41: 【17†view license†creativecommons.org】\nL42: \nL43: ### Additional Features\nL44: \nL45:   * 【18†Audio Summary】\nL46: ### Current browse context:\nL47: \nL48: cs.AR\nL49: \nL50: 【19†< prev】   |   【20†next >】\nL51: \nL52: 【21†new】 | 【22†recent】 | 【23†2026-10】\nL53: \nL54: Change to browse by:\nL55: \nL56: 【24†cs】\nL57: \nL58: ### References & Citations\nL59: \nL60:   * 【25†SciX†scixplorer.org】\nL61:   * 【26†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com】\nL62:   * 【27†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.or